### 5. Installation — own Drive client_id + setup command + dry-run exit

- **Type:** Simple skill execution + external wait
- **Intent:** Require each operator to create and use their **own** Google OAuth
  **`client_id` / `client_secret`** (Desktop app) before finishing rclone Drive
  remote setup; keep credentials on the user’s machine; exit installation with a
  dry-run result.
- **Inputs:** Provider + remote path from §4; OS; hosting **`localPath`**;
  optional existing rclone remote name when reconnecting a prior remote.
- **Expected output:** Own-`client_id` guidance + copy-pasteable setup command (or
  short script body); user confirmation that setup finished successfully;
  **dry-run log** (command, exit code, pass/fail summary) as the **installation
  exit result**.
- **Next-step resolution:** Dry-run **pass** → §6 (promote hosting worktree to
  main). Dry-run **fail** or setup abort → USER_CHECKPOINT below, then §6 / §7
  or §9.

**Why own Google Drive `client_id` (binding — always state briefly before setup):**

rclone’s **shared** Google Drive `client_id` is **being retired and will stop
working during 2026**. Leaving `client_id` / `client_secret` blank in
`rclone config` uses that shared id and will interrupt sync later. Operators
**must** create their own OAuth client and enter it when configuring the remote.
Canonical guide: [Making your own client_id](https://rclone.org/drive/#making-your-own-client-id)
(Google Cloud project → enable Google Drive API → OAuth consent → create **Desktop**
OAuth client → note `client_id` and `client_secret`).

**Forbidden:** telling the user it is OK to leave `client_id` blank for new
Drive remotes; treating the rclone NOTICE about the shared id as ignorable noise
when configuring or reconnecting.

**Why the user must run this command (binding — always state briefly before the gate):**

rclone remote setup for Google Drive opens a **browser OAuth / credential flow**
(or prompts for secrets, including `client_id` / `client_secret`). The Mission
Control agent **cannot** complete that interactive auth on the user’s behalf.
Running the command in **their** terminal keeps credentials on their machine and
lets them finish browser consent.

**How to open the terminal (instruct every time this step runs):**

| Platform | Primary | Fallback |
|----------|---------|----------|
| Windows / Linux | **Ctrl+`** (backtick) — Toggle Integrated Terminal | **View → Terminal**, or Command Palette → **View: Toggle Terminal** |
| macOS | **Ctrl+`** (same default) | **View → Terminal**, or Command Palette → **View: Toggle Terminal** |

**Do not** recommend **Ctrl+J** / **Cmd+J** as the terminal shortcut — that toggles
the **panel** and may show Problems or Output instead of Terminal.
**Do not** recommend Explorer / Finder double-click as the primary path.

**New remote setup (binding):**

1. Point the user to create their own `client_id` / `client_secret` (link above)
   **before** or as the first prompts of `rclone config`.
2. Generate the OS-appropriate setup command (or short script) for copy/paste into
   the integrated terminal. The command path **must** include entering non-empty
   `client_id` and `client_secret` when creating the Google Drive remote — **do
   not** instruct them to leave those fields blank.
3. Present the command for copy/paste.

**Existing remote already using the shared id (binding when reconnecting):**

When the user already has a Drive remote (for example after a sync that printed
the shared-`client_id` NOTICE):

1. Have them inspect with `rclone config show <remote>:` (blank/missing
   `client_id` / `client_secret` means shared id).
2. Guide them to set their own `client_id` / `client_secret` on that remote
   (edit via `rclone config` or documented config fields).
3. Require `rclone config reconnect <remote>:` and answer **Y** to refresh the
   token so rclone actually uses the new client — omitting reconnect leaves the
   old token/shared-id path in effect.
4. One Google Cloud OAuth client may be reused across remotes; each remote still
   needs its own reconnect after the client fields are set.

USER_CHECKPOINT — #external-wait — user ran setup (or reconnect) in integrated terminal.

Open structured choice **before** ending the turn: **setup succeeded** · **retry
command** · **abort** · **More details for option _**. Recap must include: short
“why own `client_id`”, “why you run this”, and the **Ctrl+`** / menu instructions.

**After the user selects setup succeeded** (same installation step — auto-advance):

1. Run a non-destructive dry-run (for example `rclone lsd` / `rclone check` /
   documented dry sync flags for this center — prefer center skill helpers when
   present; otherwise use rclone docs consistent with
   [`.sedea/centers/documentation-management/rules/00_documentation-management.mdc`](../../rules/00_documentation-management.mdc)).
2. Report the dry-run result. **Installation exits with that dry-run result** —
   there is no separate dry-run protocol section.

USER_CHECKPOINT — retry dry-run · fix remote · abort after dry-run failure.

### 6. Promote hosting worktree to main

- **Type:** Simple skill execution + external wait
- **Intent:** Land hosting-repo tracked edits on **`main`** before full pull.
- **Inputs:** Hosting **`WORKTREE_ROOT`** from §3 (or earlier) when tracked files
  changed; developer pipeline consent.
- **Expected output:** PR merged; worktree cleaned up; **`HOSTING_ROOT`** on
  updated **`main`**.
- **Next-step resolution:** Success or skip-no-changes → §7 (full pull). Abort →
  USER_CHECKPOINT below.

When this pass has **no** uncommitted/unmerged hosting-repo tracked changes,
**skip** this section and continue to §7.

Otherwise run **Hosting-repo ship cadence** end-to-end on **this pass’s**
**`WORKTREE_ROOT`** only:

1. Rule **6** pipeline (commit / push / PR) after developer pipeline-depth consent.
2. Inline **`pr-review`** until **`continuationStatus: terminal`**.
3. **#external-wait** — developer merges on GitHub.
4. Inline **`worktree-cleanup`** (MCP detach when mounted, then center
   **`worktree-cleanup.sh`**) for **this** path only — Path A or Path B ownership.
5. On **`HOSTING_ROOT`**: **`git pull origin main`** so the primary clone matches
   merged **`main`**.

**Forbidden:** full pull (§7) while this pass still has an unmerged hosting
worktree PR for the configure edit set; agent-delegated approve+merge; removing
worktrees you do not own.

USER_CHECKPOINT — #external-wait — review/merge PR, then resume cleanup · check PR status · defer full pull · abort.

USER_CHECKPOINT — retry ship · skip to full pull (document risk) · abort after ship failure.

